{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1091604-wards-i-m-having-trouble-figuring-out-how-they-work-now","id":1091604,"title":"WARDS -- I'm having trouble figuring out how they work now.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-12T10:14:58-07:00","id":1091604,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> WARDS -- I'm having trouble figuring out how they work now.","content":"Before <i>How Do You DO That </i>(&quot;HDYDT&quot;), if I was going to ward a technocracy or tradition stronghold against scrying/remote viewing, teleportation, and other spacial trickery, I would basically construct a straight Correspondence effect that ostensibly prevented <i>outside</i> attempted uses of Correspondence within the bounds of the ward, and would assign successes to size (of the area being warded), duration of the ward, and <i>effectiveness</i>. With regard to effectiveness, I conceived that there was no inherent upper limit on successes (beyond simply the limit of how many successes any given mage can put into an effect), such that if one had ten Virtual Adepts with Arete 4/Corr. 4 apiece, and they in turn each had 5-10 acolytes, you could produce a ward against scrying/teleportation/miscellaneous-spatial-shenanigans with an effectiveness in the dozens, if not hundreds of successes ([Arete 4 x Willpower 5 x 10 mages] + between 10 and 20 acolyte successes between all 10 mages). In this construction, one would have to surpass or exceed these successes before they could effectively penetrate the ward with Correspondence for any given purpose. And what's more, if I were going to ward the area against people or squirrels, the effect would work the same way except that you would add Life 3; if you were gonna ward it against plutonium, you'd add Matter at some level; if you were gonna ward against spirits, you'd incorporate Spirit--etc. But the underlying principle was that you would spend successes on size, duration, and then as many successes on &quot;effect&quot; or &quot;potency&quot; as you could effectively muster.<br /> <br /> Now comes <i>How Do You DO That</i>, and I'm <i>hella</i> confused. First, I understand and appreciate the way they go into what the minimum cost of different wards are based on a bevy of factors (i.e., a permanent major ward with that bans four objectives, where one of the objectives is a particular paradigm = minimum X number of successes using the costs listed in the book). I'm super okay with that; I'm also cool with the idea that it costs quintessence (though I may not use that in my game since we <i>haven't </i>been playing with that).<br /> <br /> My <b>first issue</b> is with this language:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>Wards CANNOT be set to block whole Spheres. Whole Spheres cannot be objectives of a ward. </i><b>HDYDT, p.96</b> </div> </div> </div>The chapter goes through all of these pains to describe how different spheres can be used to block different things--but how do you block someone from <i>scrying </i>in if you can't just straight up block Correspondence? What sphere would you even use to block someone's ability to use Corr. to depict (onto or through whatever surface of their choosing) a real time image of the interior of the warded area? I understand that teleportation by unauthorized persons is prevented by the ward by dint of the fact that that teleportation necessarily involves an attempt to BE in the warded area, and therefore a ward that bans such a presence would kick in. But it's not like when you scry you're necessarily co-locating your actual eyes into the scryed area--on the contrary, I would say that scrying typically works via a sort of a hand-waved abstraction: i.e., the depiction of the scryed area simple manifests upon whatever vessel the caster desires. So how would you stop that? What is the &quot;objective&quot; being warded, and what sphere are you tacking on to designate it effectively?<br /> <br /> My <b>second issue </b>concerns the calculation of the cost of the ward. Specifically, the book does not explicitly make an allowance for spending successes to make the Ward difficult to dismantle; instead there's just the bare minimum cost to establish the ward and that seems to be it. In other words, at best, you're almost never going to have a ward so powerful that that like three starting mages with Arete 3 apiece aren't going to be able to get through by simply preparing a whopper of a teleportation effect the successes of which would exceed the ward's.<br /> <br /> My <b>third issue </b>is with the description of &quot;The Ban&quot; (<i>HDYDT, p.100</i>). What does it actually look like when a mage wards their sanctum against John Courage (to use a name from the book itself)? Would their hand just stop when they reached the threshold of the warded area? Would it <i>feel</i> like anything (i.e., like a solid wall; like resistance you feel when you try to put two magnets together)? This is almost more of a <i>storytelling </i>question because the actual systematic effect is easy: the warded-against object cannot enter the area <i>period</i>. Full Stop. But what the hell would that manifest as? What would that look like? And if you punched the edge of the ward beyond which you could not actually move, how hard would it feel? I, in real life, can no more walk <i>through</i> the wall of a bounce house any more than I could through a brick wall--but punching one would <i>not</i> be the same as punching the other.<br /> <br /> My<b> fourth and final issue </b>concerns &quot;Co-Occuptational Wards&quot; (<i>HDYDT, p.100</i>). If I ward my house against John Courage, such that he can't interact with the house, could he not simply just walk <i>through</i> the house to observe what's going on within? I mean, I get that this is probably more a ward meant to stop unauthorized people form taking a mage's personal possessions, that would also theoretically mean that anyone so warded could look INSIDE of the warded object or person to see what's within. Is that just a quirky way that this works?<br /> <br /> On a parting note, I'd be interested to know how you all might construct the wards for a high-level Technocratic stronghold. What would you ward against, what kinds of wards would you use, and how many successes and quint would it take to make such a ward (more or less, doesn't need to be exact).<br /> <br /> Thank you in advance to all who chime in!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":2319,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","user":"CaptOtter","date":". 05-12-2017, 01:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T11:03:28-07:00","id":1091621,"title":"","content":"A protection against Scrying isn't the same as a Correspondence Ward. This kind of protection it's defined in the text of Correspondence 2, and it's more like Mind 1 shield, or the Time 2 power that blocks time perception. You use Correspondence 2 to block &quot;connections&quot; correspondence uses, which causes a sort of &quot;countermagic&quot; against attempts to scry (each one of your successes cancels a success of the mage who is scrying).<br /> <br /> A Ward, as per HDYDT isn't of that type. They don't add a buffer of successes you need to overcome to pass the ward (unless you try to dispell the effect, which it's countermagick), they just block pass entirely or give you penalties if you pass. Blocking Correspondence itself this way would prevent *any* use of the sphere regardless of the number of successes accrued, such a power it's <i>extraordinary </i>(It's very rare in Mage to completely remove the power to use a Sphere).<br /> <br /> So, basically, I treat them as magicks of different kinds.<br /> <br /> About your second issue: You can always spend more sucesses as per the basic rules. HDYDT doesn't replace the basic rules, it assumes those rules and sometimes expand uppon them and/or overrides them Thus, if the basic rules says you can do that, and HDYDT doesn't explicitly says otherwise, then you can do that.<br /> <br /> (Add mandatory advice about treating HDYDT as a useful bunch of houserules. That book should simplify using magick, if it makes it harder don't use it).<br /> <br /> About your third issue: How magick &quot;feels&quot; it's usually dependant on the mage's Paradigm. This isn't new, it's just like how you can perceive Quintessencece as light, sound, wind, or whatever. This aesthetial stuff it's almost always handled as &quot;it feels as the player thinks it should feel&quot; (the only exception being where this feeling should have mechanical implications-a ward that makes enemies roll with pain would need a mechanic and Mind)<br /> <br /> About the fourth: Yes, John Courage can enter trough the Warded walls of your home and kill you. But look at the bright side, your house it's intact.<br /> <br /> This kind of ward can be quite interesting, for instance, you could Ward yourself against walls, and then pass through every wall without being immaterial. You could ward against undead and walk trough an army of zombies, burning them with your magick (while a Correspondence 4 *bubble* would allow zombies to &quot;push&quot; you arround or *grab* you)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-12-2017, 02:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T11:11:59-07:00","id":1091626,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1091604-wards-i-m-having-trouble-figuring-out-how-they-work-now?p=1091621#post1091621\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A protection against Scrying isn't the same as a Correspondence Ward. This kind of protection it's defined in the text of Correspondence 2, and it's more like Mind 1 shield, or the Time 2 power that blocks time perception. You use Correspondence 2 to block &quot;connections&quot; correspondence uses, which causes a sort of &quot;countermagic&quot; against attempts to scry (each one of your successes cancels a success of the mage who is scrying).</div> </div> </div> </div>Holy smokes this makes a <i>whole</i> lot of sense. Thank you!<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T16:50:26-07:00","id":1091761,"title":"","content":"Honestly. The more I hear about it, the more I feel like HDYDT isn't worth the paper it's printed on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T17:18:07-07:00","id":1091772,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Enginseer-42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1091604-wards-i-m-having-trouble-figuring-out-how-they-work-now?p=1091761#post1091761\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly. The more I hear about it, the more I feel like HDYDT isn't worth the paper it's printed on.</div> </div> </div> </div>It has some neat stuff but really it's all optional rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 201